1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,306
A bus pulls away from a stop, its speed rising steadily: five metres per second every second.

2
00:00:07,306 --> 00:00:08,647
How far has it gone?

3
00:00:08,647 --> 00:00:11,414
The distance is the area under the speed graph.

4
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:14,968
The area so far is five halves t squared.

5
00:00:14,968 --> 00:00:19,252
And differentiate that: five t, the speed.

6
00:00:19,252 --> 00:00:23,444
So the area function is the thing whose derivative is the speed.

7
00:00:23,444 --> 00:00:29,696
Finding it means running differentiation backwards: anti-differentiation, or integration.

8
00:00:35,167 --> 00:00:40,284
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 13: Anti-derivatives.

9
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:43,840
Seven parts.

10
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:45,546
The standard integrals.

11
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:48,645
Substitution and integration by parts.

12
00:00:48,645 --> 00:00:52,252
The standard forms with a squared plus or minus x squared.

13
00:00:52,252 --> 00:00:53,775
Partial fractions.

14
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:56,601
The definite integral and its properties.

15
00:00:56,601 --> 00:00:59,296
The integral as the limit of a sum.

16
00:00:59,296 --> 00:01:01,595
And areas under and between curves.

17
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:12,237
If the derivative of x squared is two x, then the anti-derivative of two x is x squared.

18
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:19,750
But so is x squared plus one, or plus anything: they are all the same curve shifted up, with identical slopes.

19
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:23,042
So every indefinite integral carries a plus C.

20
00:01:24,267 --> 00:01:28,748
Read your table of derivatives backwards and you have a table of integrals.

21
00:01:28,748 --> 00:01:38,292
Raise the power by one and divide by the new power, except for one over x, which gives log x. e to the x is its own integral.

22
00:01:38,292 --> 00:01:42,771
Sine integrates to minus cosine, cosine to sine.

23
00:01:42,771 --> 00:01:47,215
And the inverse trig derivatives give inverse trig integrals.

24
00:01:48,433 --> 00:01:49,172
Term by term.

25
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:54,474
Three x squared gives x cubed; four over x gives four log x.

26
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:00,992
Write root x as x to the half: raise to three halves and divide by three halves.

27
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,350
Collect, with one constant.

28
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:06,309
Check by differentiating: you get back the integrand.

29
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,586
Substitution is the chain rule run backwards.

30
00:02:15,586 --> 00:02:18,217
Look for a function together with its derivative.

31
00:02:18,217 --> 00:02:27,544
In x root of x squared plus one, the inside, x squared plus one, has derivative two x, and an x is sitting outside.

32
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:30,112
So let u be x squared plus one.

33
00:02:30,112 --> 00:02:37,026
Then x d x is d u over two, and the integral becomes root u over two: easy.

34
00:02:37,026 --> 00:02:42,143
Put x back at the end: a third of x squared plus one, to the three halves.

35
00:02:43,367 --> 00:02:44,625
A clever substitution.

36
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:48,790
Multiply top and bottom by sec x plus tan x.

37
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,036
Now the top is exactly the derivative of the bottom.

38
00:02:52,267 --> 00:02:56,806
And derivative over function integrates to log of the function.

39
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:05,905
For a product of two different kinds of function, use integration by parts: the product rule backwards.

40
00:03:05,905 --> 00:03:21,379
The integral of u d v is u v minus the integral of v d u. Choose u as the factor that gets simpler when differentiated; the rule ILATE gives the order: inverse trig, log, algebraic, trig, exponential.

41
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,458
For x e to the x: x is algebraic, so it is u.

42
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:33,487
Then the new integral is just e to the x, and the answer is x e to the x minus e to the x.

43
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,840
The cyclic case: neither factor simplifies.

44
00:03:38,067 --> 00:03:40,757
Parts once, with u equal to sin x.

45
00:03:40,967 --> 00:03:43,065
Parts again on the new integral.

46
00:03:43,065 --> 00:03:45,548
The original integral I reappears.

47
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:49,597
So I appears on both sides: move it across and divide by two.

48
00:03:55,167 --> 00:04:00,312
A family of standard forms is built around a squared plus or minus x squared.

49
00:04:00,312 --> 00:04:04,244
One over x squared plus a squared gives inverse tan.

50
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:08,710
One over root of a squared minus x squared gives inverse sine.

51
00:04:08,710 --> 00:04:10,820
And others give logarithms.

52
00:04:10,820 --> 00:04:17,937
To use them, first make the coefficient of x squared equal to one, and complete the square if there is an x term.

53
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:20,134
Matching the forms.

54
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:27,163
Take the four out: a is three halves, giving a sixth of inverse tan two x over three.

55
00:04:27,367 --> 00:04:32,659
Here a is four directly: an eighth of log of x minus four over x plus four.

56
00:04:32,867 --> 00:04:37,874
Take two out of the root: a half inverse sine two x over three.

57
00:04:39,900 --> 00:04:42,336
The last form has a lovely picture.

58
00:04:42,336 --> 00:04:48,508
The area under the quarter circle from nought to x splits into a triangle and a sector.

59
00:04:48,508 --> 00:04:53,860
The triangle has area half x times root of a squared minus x squared.

60
00:04:53,860 --> 00:05:01,269
The sector's angle is inverse sine of x over a, so its area is a squared over two times that angle.

61
00:05:01,269 --> 00:05:03,326
That is exactly the formula.

62
00:05:03,326 --> 00:05:07,206
Algebraically, substitute x equals a sin theta.

63
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,759
A fraction with a factorised denominator can be split into simpler fractions, each easy to integrate.

64
00:05:19,759 --> 00:05:26,506
One over x minus one times x plus two is A over x minus one, plus B over x plus two.

65
00:05:26,733 --> 00:05:28,811
Multiply through by the denominator.

66
00:05:29,033 --> 00:05:35,988
Choose clever values of x. Put x equal to one and the B term vanishes: A is a third.

67
00:05:35,988 --> 00:05:39,492
Put x equal to minus two: B is minus a third.

68
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:43,051
Each piece integrates to a log, and the logs combine.

69
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:46,274
The form depends on the factors.

70
00:05:46,274 --> 00:05:48,891
A linear factor gets a constant on top.

71
00:05:48,891 --> 00:05:52,797
A repeated factor gets one fraction for each power.

72
00:05:52,797 --> 00:05:56,313
An irreducible quadratic gets a linear numerator.

73
00:05:56,313 --> 00:06:00,799
And if the top is not of lower degree than the bottom, divide first.

74
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,610
An irreducible quadratic.

75
00:06:03,833 --> 00:06:06,787
x squared plus one needs a linear numerator.

76
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,063
x equal to one gives A; compare the x squared terms and the constants for B and D.

77
00:06:13,267 --> 00:06:16,951
The last fraction splits into a log and an inverse tan.

78
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:32,222
The definite integral from a to b is F of b minus F of a, where F is any anti-derivative; the constant cancels.

79
00:06:32,222 --> 00:06:33,941
It measures signed area.

80
00:06:33,941 --> 00:06:38,677
Integrate sine from nought to pi: the green hump has area exactly two.

81
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:43,003
Carry on past pi, and the curve dips below the axis.

82
00:06:43,003 --> 00:06:53,037
That area counts as negative, so the integral falls: to one at three pi over two, and to zero at two pi, where the two humps cancel exactly.

83
00:06:54,267 --> 00:06:57,055
Three properties do most of the work.

84
00:06:57,055 --> 00:07:01,847
Swapping the limits changes the sign, and you can split at any point.

85
00:07:01,847 --> 00:07:08,461
Replacing x by a minus x does not change the integral from nought to a: the reflection trick.

86
00:07:08,461 --> 00:07:14,983
And over a symmetric interval, an odd function integrates to zero, an even one to twice the half.

87
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:18,584
No anti-derivative exists here.

88
00:07:18,584 --> 00:07:19,973
Use the reflection trick.

89
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:25,121
Replace x by pi over two minus x: sine and cosine swap.

90
00:07:25,333 --> 00:07:31,699
Add the two forms: the fractions add to one, and the integral of one is pi over two.

91
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,000
So I is pi over four.

92
00:07:35,133 --> 00:07:35,801
The trick again.

93
00:07:36,367 --> 00:07:43,133
Replace x by pi minus x: the integral equals pi times the integral of sin x, minus I.

94
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:47,644
The integral of sin x from nought to pi is two, so I is pi.

95
00:07:47,644 --> 00:07:49,551
Integration by parts agrees.

96
00:07:55,133 --> 00:07:56,853
Why is an integral an area?

97
00:07:56,853 --> 00:08:03,116
Fill the region under x squared from nought to one with n thin rectangles.

98
00:08:03,116 --> 00:08:05,773
Their total area overshoots a little.

99
00:08:05,773 --> 00:08:08,023
Now take more and thinner rectangles.

100
00:08:08,233 --> 00:08:17,518
The sum uses the sum of squares from unit four: n, n plus one, two n plus one, over six n cubed.

101
00:08:17,518 --> 00:08:27,454
As n grows, the extra terms vanish and the sum tends to one third: exactly the integral, x cubed over three from nought to one.

102
00:08:32,233 --> 00:08:39,813
To find the area between two curves, find where they meet: x squared equals two x at nought and two.

103
00:08:39,813 --> 00:08:43,831
Decide which is on top: at x equals one the line is higher.

104
00:08:44,033 --> 00:08:46,834
Each thin strip has height top minus bottom.

105
00:08:46,834 --> 00:08:52,407
Add the strips: integrate two x minus x squared from nought to two.

106
00:08:52,407 --> 00:08:53,901
Four thirds square units.

107
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:56,262
The circle, proved.

108
00:08:56,467 --> 00:08:59,381
Four times the quarter circle, using the standard form.

109
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:04,523
At x equals a: inverse sine of one is pi over two.

110
00:09:04,523 --> 00:09:05,324
Pi a squared.

111
00:09:05,533 --> 00:09:09,817
The ellipse is the circle squashed by b over a: pi a b.

112
00:09:11,833 --> 00:09:12,779
Two quick areas.

113
00:09:13,067 --> 00:09:15,814
Under x squared: nine square units.

114
00:09:16,033 --> 00:09:20,475
Under one over x from one to e: exactly one.

115
00:09:20,475 --> 00:09:22,748
That is what makes e the natural base.

116
00:09:24,767 --> 00:09:25,615
A warning.

117
00:09:25,615 --> 00:09:31,852
An integral counts area below the axis as negative, but an area cannot be negative.

118
00:09:31,852 --> 00:09:36,519
Split the region where the curve crosses the axis and add the sizes.

119
00:09:38,933 --> 00:09:40,771
Pause and think about the two humps.

120
00:09:47,667 --> 00:09:51,262
Zero: the hump above and the hump below cancel.

121
00:09:51,262 --> 00:09:53,371
The total area, though, is four.

122
00:09:55,367 --> 00:09:56,228
One more.

123
00:09:56,228 --> 00:09:57,107
Use ILATE.

124
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:11,537
x, the algebraic factor: it becomes one when differentiated, and the integral that is left is easy.

125
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:14,789
Quick recap.

126
00:10:14,789 --> 00:10:24,177
Integration runs differentiation backwards, with a plus C. Substitution undoes the chain rule, parts undoes the product rule.

127
00:10:24,177 --> 00:10:28,084
The standard forms and partial fractions handle the rest.

128
00:10:28,084 --> 00:10:34,694
The definite integral is a signed area, and the area between curves is the integral of top minus bottom.

129
00:10:38,867 --> 00:10:40,718
That was anti-derivatives.

130
00:10:40,718 --> 00:10:46,147
Next, differential equations: equations that involve derivatives, and how to solve them.

131
00:10:46,147 --> 00:10:46,697
See you there.

